## Inheritance model

This project uses **soft inheritance** — at onboard time, project-
local A-rules (A011+) from the chosen sibling were copied INTO this
repo's `.claude/rules/brain-hot.md`. After that copy, the two repos
evolve **independently**:

- A new A-rule added here later does NOT auto-propagate to the
  sibling. Conversely, a sibling adding A-rules later doesn't reach
  this repo unless `/onboard refresh` is re-run with explicit
  re-inheritance.
- The inheritance is **point-in-time**, not a live link.
- The original source for each inherited rule is preserved in the
  rule body (e.g. *"inherited from ../service-billing 2026-05-22"*).

## When soft inheritance is the right choice

✓ 2-3 sibling repos owned by the same team that share most
  conventions (test policy, branch naming, deploy workflow)

✓ Teams that want to **bootstrap** a new repo's brain-hot.md from a
  sibling's lessons without committing to ongoing sync

✓ Service repos in the same business domain that share security or
  compliance constraints

✗ **Not** the right choice if you have ≥3 repos that need to stay
  in lockstep — use the org-fork pattern instead (see below)

✗ **Not** the right choice for cross-team coordination where rules
  must converge — use a shared canonical brain at the meta level

## When to upgrade to org-fork

Once 3+ repos share substantial A-rules, the soft-inheritance model
breaks down (drift accumulates faster than retros can promote). The
upgrade path: **fork `claude-flightdeck` into your org's namespace**,
add the shared A-rules to that org-fork's `core/.claude/rules/`, and
have each project re-install from the org-fork.

Full recipe: [`./multi-team-deployment.md`](./multi-team-deployment.md).

After org-fork adoption, this `sibling-repos.md` becomes mostly a
historical record — sharing happens at the template layer, not via
per-repo inheritance.

## Conflict protocol

When two siblings both add A-rules with **the same number** (e.g.
both this repo and sibling A both have an A015), the rules diverge
silently. To resolve:

1. Compare both repos' `brain-hot.md` `## Project-specific rules`
   sections side-by-side
2. Pick which definition is canonical (usually the one with the
   stronger evidence cite)
3. Renumber the loser to the next free A-number
4. Document the renumber in this file under `## Conflicts resolved`

Do NOT silently overwrite — the evidence trail in each rule's body
is the audit trail.

## Conflicts resolved

| When | Conflict | Resolution |
|---|---|---|
| _example: 2026-06-01_ | _Both repos had A015 for different rules_ | _This repo's A015 renumbered to A016; sibling kept A015_ |

## Re-running onboard with refresh

```bash
# In Claude Code, from THIS project's root:
/onboard refresh
```

`/onboard refresh` will:
- Re-detect sibling installs (the sibling list may have grown)
- Re-mine git history for new A-rule candidates (yours + optionally
  re-pull inherited rules from sibling)
- Present a DELTA: which inherited rules have changed in the sibling
  since the last sync, and which of THIS repo's rules are new
- Multi-select ratification — same as initial onboard

Do **NOT** run `/onboard` (the full wizard) on an existing install —
that's for greenfield only. `refresh` is for incremental sync.

